WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Audit trails are accountability records.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:10.920
They help answer who did what, when it happened, under which identity or authority, and what boundary decision was made.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:25.740
In sovereign AI systems, that may include user requests, service account actions, automation jobs, administrative changes, model access, data source access, exports, configuration updates, and policy exceptions.

4
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.780
Audit logs are different from ordinary operational logs.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.520
Operational logs help troubleshoot behavior: a timeout, a failed worker, or a slow model load.

6
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:47.100
Audit logs help explain responsibility and authorization: which user started a workflow, which service account accessed a source, who changed a model route, or who exported results.

7
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.900
The system should distinguish human users, services, administrators, and automation.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:02.920
A shared service account with broad privileges can make accountability weak unless actions are tied back to a workflow owner, job identity, or approval record.

9
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:11.660
Admin activity deserves especially strong tracking because it may change access boundaries, logging rules, model versions, or retention settings.

10
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.540
Permission decisions should be explainable after the fact.

11
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:23.080
If a private RAG workflow allowed or denied source access, the audit record should support review without exposing more content than necessary.

12
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:28.060
If an exception was granted, the reason and approver should be visible.

13
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.660
Audit logs need protection.

14
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:37.560
They should be access controlled, retained according to policy, resistant to tampering, and reviewed in a practical rhythm.

15
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.460
Accountability fails when audit records are either missing or broadly exposed.
